1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,700
On the interbank currency desk, the gap between two large trades

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,748
averages twenty-five minutes, with a standard deviation of about twenty-five minutes.

3
00:00:08,848 --> 00:00:11,955
Fit a normal curve with those numbers, and look to the

4
00:00:12,055 --> 00:00:15,518
left of zero. Sixteen percent of the gaps would be negative:

5
00:00:15,618 --> 00:00:18,368
the next trade would arrive before the last one.

6
00:00:19,466 --> 00:00:22,556
Waiting times live on zero to infinity and lean to

7
00:00:22,656 --> 00:00:26,001
the right. The gamma family is built for exactly that.

8
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:31,420
Here's the picture to keep in mind. Large insurance claims arrive at

9
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:35,451
random, about one every four days. The time to the first claim

10
00:00:35,551 --> 00:00:39,351
is an exponential wait. The time to the third claim is three

11
00:00:39,451 --> 00:00:43,707
such waits added together, and that is a gamma variable, with shape

12
00:00:43,807 --> 00:00:46,957
alpha equal to three and scale beta equal to four.

13
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,551
Now let alpha grow. With alpha equal to one, you wait for

14
00:00:51,651 --> 00:00:56,489
a single claim: the density starts at its highest point and decays.

15
00:00:56,589 --> 00:01:01,427
Wait for two claims, three, five, and the hump moves right, spreads

16
00:01:01,527 --> 00:01:06,513
out, and becomes more symmetric. The mean is alpha times beta, twelve

17
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:11,304
days for the third claim, and the variance is alpha beta squared.

18
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,952
Alpha equal to one is the exponential, and it

19
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:17,782
has a strange property. A bank's call centre has

20
00:01:17,882 --> 00:01:21,024
hold times that are exponential with mean four minutes.

21
00:01:21,116 --> 00:01:26,054
Suppose you have already waited five minutes. All that remains possible is the

22
00:01:26,154 --> 00:01:30,382
part of the curve beyond five. To condition on it, slide that piece

23
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:33,999
back to zero and stretch it until its area is one again.

24
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:38,113
It lands exactly on the original curve. The caller who has held

25
00:01:38,213 --> 00:01:42,309
for five minutes faces the same wait as someone who just dialled

26
00:01:42,409 --> 00:01:47,160
in. The exponential has no memory. Whether that's realistic is a modelling

27
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:50,832
question: a queue that is served in order does remember.

28
00:01:51,916 --> 00:01:55,547
One more member of the family. Set beta equal to two and alpha

29
00:01:55,647 --> 00:01:59,699
equal to nu over two, and you get the chi-square distribution with nu

30
00:01:59,799 --> 00:02:03,430
degrees of freedom. Its mean is nu and its variance is two nu.

31
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:07,762
It gets its own name because statistics uses it constantly: it will come

32
00:02:07,862 --> 00:02:10,650
back when we test variances later in the course.

33
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:16,139
So: the gamma is a waiting time. Alpha counts the events

34
00:02:16,239 --> 00:02:19,815
you wait for, and beta sets the time scale. The exponential

35
00:02:19,915 --> 00:02:23,242
waits for one event and forgets how long it has waited.

36
00:02:23,342 --> 00:02:27,043
And the chi-square is the gamma that statistics will lean on.

